            <ArticleTitle>Analysis of the prevalence of atelectasis in patients undergoing bariatric surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>577-582</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0104-0014(15)00116-5</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bjane.2014.11.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To observe the prevalence of atelectasis in patients undergoing bariatric surgery and the influence of the body mass index (BMI), gender and age on the prevalence of atelectasis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective study of 407 patients and reports on chest X-rays carried out before and after bariatric surgery over a period of 14 months. Only patients who underwent bariatric surgery by laparotomy were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was an overall prevalence of 37.84% of atelectasis, with the highest prevalence in the lung bases and with greater prevalence in women (RR=1.48). There was a ratio of 30% for the influence of age for individuals under the age of 36, and of 45% for those older than 36 (RR=0.68). There was no significant influence of BMI on the prevalence of atelectasis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The prevalence of atelectasis in bariatric surgery is 37% and the main risk factors are being female and aged over 36 years.</AbstractText>
